<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamagata Aritomo (1838–1922) was a Japanese soldier and statesman from the Chōshū domain, regarded as the father of the modern Japanese army. He introduced conscription in 1873, commanded in the First Sino-Japanese War and twice served as prime minister, in 1889–91 and 1898–1900. As one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genro">genro</a>, the elder statesmen, he dominated Japanese politics until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo.jpg" alt="Yamagata Aritomo" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamagata_Aritomo_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
